<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trans-Indochinese Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1936</span> · Also known as North–South railway (Vietnam), Transindochinois</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trans-Indochinese Railway is the metre-gauge line of about 1,700 kilometres running along the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> City. Begun under Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-doumer">Paul Doumer</a> in the 1890s and built in sections, it was completed in 1936. Heavily damaged during the wars of the following decades, it was reopened along its full length in 1976 as the North–South Railway.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trans-indochinese-railway.jpg" alt="Trans-Indochinese Railway" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gilrad rom · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VietnamRailway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
